## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical ability to extend a docker-dca setup with a compliant, low-latency data-redaction path.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config-driven per-tenant policies
- Sidecar pattern and in-memory caching
- Streaming JSON redaction without full buffering
- Nginx-edge integration and TLS context

## Code Example
```go
package main
import (
  "encoding/json"
  "fmt"
)
func redact(m map[string]interface{}, fields map[string]bool){
  for k,v := range m {
    if fields[k] { m[k] = "***REDACTED***"; continue }
    if mm, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(mm, fields) }
    if arr, ok := v.([]interface{}); ok {
      for _, it := range arr { if sub, ok := it.(map[string]interface{}); ok { redact(sub, fields) } }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test nested JSON redaction with large bodies?
- How would you monitor redaction performance without affecting latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design edge routing with policy-driven gating and cross-DC deployment, plus observability and security concerns in docker-dca.

## Key Concepts

- TLS termination at the edge with Nginx
- Central policy engine (OPA) integration
- Per-tenant feature flag evaluation
- Cross-DC routing semantics
- Observability with Prometheus and OTEL

## Code Example

```rego
package featureflag.allow
default allow = false
allow {
  input.tenant == "tenant-A"
  input.flag == "new_feature"
}
```

```nginx
# Nginx map-based routing example
http {
  map $http_x_feature_flag $upstream {
    default http://api_v1;
    "new_feature" http://api_v2;
  }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    location /api/ { proxy_pass $upstream; }
  }
}
```

